% Ultrasound FFT: Voltage(time) -> Magnitude(dB) vs Frequency(MHz)
% Input: time vector t (seconds) and voltage vector v (volts)
% Output: single-sided magnitude spectrum in dB vs frequency in MHz

% ---------- USER INPUT ----------
% Example placeholders (replace with your data loading)
% load('yourData.mat');  % should provide t and v
% t = ...;  v = ...;

% ---------- CHECKS ----------
t = t(:);
v = v(:);
assert(numel(t) == numel(v), 't and v must have the same length');
assert(all(diff(t) > 0), 't must be strictly increasing');

% ---------- SAMPLING PARAMETERS ----------
Fs = 1/mean(diff(t));          % Sampling frequency (Hz)
N  = numel(v);                 % Number of samples

% Optional: remove DC offset
v = v - mean(v);

% Optional: apply window to reduce spectral leakage
w = hann(N);
v_win = v .* w;

% FFT length (can use next power of 2 for speed)
Nfft = 2^nextpow2(N);

% ---------- FFT ----------
V = fft(v_win, Nfft);

% Frequency axis (single-sided)
f = (0:(Nfft/2))*(Fs/Nfft);    % Hz, includes DC and Nyquist (if Nfft even)

% ---------- MAGNITUDE (SINGLE-SIDED) ----------
% Amplitude correction for window + single-sided spectrum scaling
% Coherent gain of window:
CG = mean(w);                  % coherent gain for amplitude correction

P2 = abs(V)/ (N * CG);         % two-sided amplitude spectrum (approx.)
P1 = P2(1:Nfft/2+1);           % single-sided
P1(2:end-1) = 2*P1(2:end-1);   % conserve energy/amplitude for single-sided

% ---------- CONVERT TO dB ----------
% If you want dB relative to 1 V (dBV):
mag_dB = 20*log10(P1 + eps);

% If you instead want dB normalized to the peak (0 dB at max), use:
% mag_dB = 20*log10(P1 / max(P1) + eps);

% Convert frequency to MHz
f_MHz = f / 1e6;

% ---------- PLOT ----------
figure;
plot(f_MHz, mag_dB, 'LineWidth', 1.2);
grid on;
xlabel('Frequency (MHz)');
ylabel('Magnitude (dB)');
title('Ultrasound Spectrum (Voltage \rightarrow Magnitude(dB) vs Frequency)');

% Optional: limit x-axis (set as needed)
% xlim([0 20]);  % e.g., 0–20 MHz
```

If you tell me (1) your sampling rate or your time units, and (2) whether you want **dBV**, **dB re 1 µV**, or **dB normalized to peak**, I can tailor the dB scaling exactly to your convention.

